      "problem": "If $\\frac{4pq+5}{5}=\\frac{4-3q}{3},$ then $q=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We need to isolate all terms with $q$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4pq+5}{5} & =\\frac{4-3q}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (4pq+5) & =5\\cdot (4-3q) \\\\ 12pq+15 & =20-15q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $15q$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12pq+15 & =20-15q \\\\ 12pq+15+15q & =20-15q+15q \\\\ 12pq+15+15q & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $15$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12pq+15+15q & =20 \\\\ 12pq+15+15q-15 & =20-15 \\\\ 12pq+15q & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $q$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $q$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $q$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12pq+15q & =5 \\\\ (12p+15)q & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(12p+15)$ to isolate $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12p+15)q & =5 \\\\ \\frac{(12p+15)q}{12p+15} & =\\frac{5}{12p+15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(12p+15)}q}{\\cancel{12p+15}} & =\\frac{5}{12p+15} \\\\ q & =\\frac{5}{12p+15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $12p+15\\ne 0.$",
